// REJECTS: Unexpected token
//
// `binding: X;` with ONE semicolon. The rule is
//     serviceBindingOrContent : (serviceBinding | serviceContentType) SEMI_COLON
// and serviceBinding ALREADY ends in SEMI_COLON of its own, so the only spelling that parses
// is `binding: fixture::hs::Binding;;` -- two of them, as written in
// ../fixtures/t2-hostedservice.pure. serviceContentType has no trailing SEMI_COLON, so its
// sibling `contentType: '...';` takes exactly one.
//
// Nobody writes a double semicolon on purpose, which is exactly why it needs pinning: a
// rewrite normalises the two arms, accepts one semicolon for both, and every existing
// HostedService in the wild -- all of which carry two -- then fails to parse. The asymmetry
// is not a typo in the .g4; it is load-bearing for real files.
//
// The rest of this element is minimal and valid: pattern, ownership and function are the
// three required fields (HostedServiceTreeWalker:98,100,106).
###HostedService
HostedService fixture::neg::SingleSemicolonBinding
{
   pattern: '/hs';
   ownership: Deployment { identifier: '1' };
   function: fixture::neg::f():String[1];
   binding: fixture::neg::Binding;
}
